{%- comment -%}
  Metro Timeline — all canvas geometry is computed client-side.

  transform.js is a pure data normalizer (events with start_min/end_min,
  owner/co_owners, side, track styling; weather milestones; now_min). The
  <script> below does everything that depends on the real rendered size:

    1. Orientation — "auto" runs the timeline left-to-right on a landscape
       canvas and top-to-bottom on a portrait one (or the setting forces one).
    2. Time window — if the canvas can't give every hour enough pixels, the
       visible window narrows around "now" and events outside it are
       summarised as "+N earlier / +N later" at the ends of the line.
    3. Spine — every track's line runs the full length of the canvas in
       one bundle. The hour axis runs down the MIDDLE of the bundle (work
       lines on one side of it, family lines on the other), so no branch
       ever has to cross the hour labels.
    4. Stations + branches — each event is a ring on its owner's line at
       its true start time. A branch leaves at 45°, runs along a "lane"
       parallel to the spine carrying the label, and — if the event is
       long enough — comes back at 45° to rejoin the line at its end time.
       Short events end in a stub instead.
    5. Lane placement — labels are measured (real DOM boxes), then each
       event takes the innermost lane where neither its label nor its
       line collides with what's already there. Same-owner branches that
       overlap in a lane become ONE spur with several rings feeding into
       it (the label just slides along it); a title that would overrun
       the end of the axis loses its time tag, then gets an ellipsis, and
       only then branches backward; when nothing fits the event is counted
       as "+N more". Text steps down a size ladder (drop location → smaller
       title → drop time) only as far as needed to keep every event.
    6. Spine position — the bundle is placed so the side that needs more
       lanes gets more room, instead of always sitting dead-centre.

  Labels are plain text on the canvas (no cards, pills, or borders).
  Colors come from TRMNLPaint.stroke(hue) so they follow the device/theme;
  every line also has its own dash pattern so it stays identifiable on a
  1-bit panel.
{%- endcomment -%}

{% template main %}

<div class="layout layout--col gap--none metro-root">

  <div class="metro-header flex flex--col gap--xsmall px--3 py--2 flex-none">
    <div class="metro-header-top flex flex--row flex--center-y flex--wrap gap--medium">
      <!-- The plugin's mark, inlined because the device renders this template
           on its own and cannot fetch repo files. Keep in step with
           assets/icon.svg: same geometry, same single-fill structure; the
           mask id is namespaced so a mashup rendering several views cannot
           collide on it.
           Drawn in the theme's own ink rather than in the brand orange. The
           panels are 1-bit and 4-bit greyscale: #e05b44 has a luma right on
           the middle, so it dithered to a 50% checkerboard and the mark came
           out as noise at every size it is drawn at. It is also the one
           thing on a dark board that would have stayed light. -->
      <svg class="metro-mark no-shrink" viewBox="0 0 512 512" role="img" aria-label="Metro Calendar" fill="currentColor">
        <mask id="metro-mark-cut">
          <rect width="512" height="512" fill="#fff"/>
          <g stroke="#000" stroke-width="38" stroke-linecap="round" fill="none">
            <path d="M96 160 H416"/>
            <path d="M96 256 H416" stroke-dasharray="60 52"/>
            <path d="M96 352 H416" stroke-dasharray="1 76"/>
            <path d="M320 160 V352" stroke-width="26" stroke-dasharray="26 34"/>
          </g>
          <g fill="#000"><circle cx="320" cy="160" r="52"/><circle cx="320" cy="256" r="52"/><circle cx="320" cy="352" r="52"/></g>
          <g fill="#fff"><circle cx="320" cy="160" r="22"/><circle cx="320" cy="256" r="22"/><circle cx="320" cy="352" r="22"/></g>
        </mask>
        <circle cx="256" cy="256" r="240" mask="url(#metro-mark-cut)"/>
      </svg>
      <span class="metro-title title title--large font--bold">{% if metro.title_word %}{{ metro.title_word }}{% else %}{{ metro.i18n.today | default: "Today" }}{% endif %}</span>
      {% if metro.date_label %}<span class="metro-date metro-pill label label--base font--bold">{{ metro.date_label }}</span>{% endif %}
      {%- comment -%}
        The window the board covers ("06:00 23:00") used to be stated as a
        pill here. The hour scale directly below says the same thing and
        says it in the right place, so the pill was a second answer to a
        question nobody had asked. window_label still travels in the
        payload: the smaller views have no scale to read it off.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="grow"></div>
      {% if metro.header_weather.hi %}
        <span class="metro-weather flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--xsmall">
          {% if metro.header_weather.icon %}<img class="metro-wicon image--adaptive" src="{{ metro.header_weather.icon }}">{% endif %}
          <span class="metro-temp title title--base">{{ metro.header_weather.hi }}&deg;<span class="metro-quiet"> / {{ metro.header_weather.lo }}&deg;</span></span>
          {% assign rain_pct = metro.i18n.rain_pct | default: "{n}% rain" | replace: "{n}", metro.header_weather.rain_chance %}
          <span class="metro-cond label label--small metro-quiet">{{ metro.header_weather.condition }} &middot; {{ rain_pct }}</span>
        </span>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
    {%- comment -%}
      Two things the board cannot say for itself. A line missing because its
      feed has been unreachable for hours looks exactly like a line whose
      owner has nothing on today, and a forecast replayed from the last
      good fetch looks exactly like a fresh one. Both are stated here
      rather than guessed at from a gap in the map.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% if metro.calendars_down and metro.calendars_down.size > 0 %}
      {%- comment -%}
        Built with assign, not written inline: a "{n}" placeholder inside an
        output tag ends the tag early and takes the whole template with it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign down_names = metro.calendars_down | join: ", " -%}
      {%- assign feed_down_tpl = metro.i18n.feed_down | default: "{n} unavailable" -%}
      {%- assign feed_down_msg = feed_down_tpl | replace: "{n}", down_names -%}
      <div class="metro-alerts label label--small metro-quiet">{{ feed_down_msg }}</div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if metro.weather_stale %}
      <div class="metro-alerts label label--small metro-quiet">{{ metro.i18n.weather_stale | default: "Forecast may be out of date" }}</div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if metro.all_day and metro.all_day.size > 0 %}
      <div class="metro-allday flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--small flex--wrap">
        {% for a in metro.all_day %}
          <span class="flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--xsmall">
            <svg class="metro-allday-swatch no-shrink" data-hue="{{ a.hue }}" data-style="{{ a.track_style }}" width="20" height="8" viewBox="0 0 20 8"></svg>
            <span class="label label--small font--bold">{{ a.title }}</span>
          </span>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    {% endif %}
  </div>

  <div class="divider divider--on-white flex-none"></div>

  <div class="metro-canvas relative grow w--full">
    <svg class="metro-svg absolute"></svg>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    The service banner. A sibling of the canvas rather than a band reserved
    inside it: the canvas is the flex child that grows, so a footer here
    takes its height off the canvas and the script, which measures the
    canvas's own box, adapts with no arithmetic of its own and no second
    place for the two to disagree. When nothing is breached transform.js
    sends null, this renders nothing at all, and the map has the height
    back.
    Solid ink, paper text: this is the one thing on the board that is not
    part of the map, and it has to read as an interruption rather than as
    another label. Wrapping is allowed because the translations are not the
    same length and a German banner that would not fit on one line should
    take two rather than be cut.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% if metro.service_alert %}
    <div class="metro-banner flex-none w--full px--3 py--1 label label--small font--bold"
         data-metro-alert="{{ metro.service_alert.kind }}">{{ metro.service_alert.text }}</div>
  {% endif %}

<style>
  /* Ink and paper only. Spacing and centring come from the framework's own
     utility classes on the element instead, because the inline-style lint
     counts a handful of property names by scanning the raw markup for them,
     comments included, against a budget of six for the whole template.
     Naming one here costs the same as using it. */
  .metro-banner {
    background: var(--framework-semantic-text-primary-text-color, #000);
    color: var(--framework-semantic-canvas-bg-color, #fff);
  }
  .metro-canvas { overflow: hidden; }
  /* the drawing surface fills its container; kept here rather than inline so
     the markup carries no style attribute of its own */
  .metro-svg { overflow: visible; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; pointer-events: none; }
  /* No box behind a label — an opaque panel over a metro map reads as a
     hole punched in it, and on a busy board those holes are most of the
     picture. The text carries its own thin paper outline instead: it stays
     legible where a line runs under it, and where nothing does there is
     nothing to see. paint-order puts the stroke BEHIND the glyphs, so the
     letterforms keep their real weight instead of being thinned by a
     centred stroke. */
  .metro-label, .metro-terminus, .metro-hour, .metro-axis-note, .metro-sky {
    paint-order: stroke fill;
    -webkit-text-stroke: 3px var(--framework-semantic-canvas-bg-color, #fff);
  }
  /* A CAPTION IS ONE THING, NOT A STACK OF ROWS.
     The time and the title are two lines of the same name, so they are set
     as tight as two lines can be set: at 1.2 the gap between them read as
     the gap between two captions, which on a board where a dozen names lean
     past each other is exactly the wrong thing for it to read as. Tighter
     also makes every label thinner, and a label's thickness is what the
     lane ladder is built out of. */
  .metro-label { line-height: 1.05; }
  .metro-hour, .metro-axis-note { white-space: nowrap; }
  .metro-header--compact .metro-window, .metro-header--compact .metro-cond { display: none; }
  /* the alert lines are the first thing to go when the header is squeezed:
     they are an explanation, and a squeezed header has no room to explain */
  .metro-header--compact .metro-alerts { display: none; }
  /* A tiny view (a quadrant, a half-horizontal on an OG panel) already
     hides the date and the weather for want of room, which leaves a band of
     paper carrying the mark and the word "Today" and nothing else. On a
     quadrant that band is a third of the board. The hour scale underneath
     says which day this is by saying what time it is, so the header goes
     and the map gets the height. */
  .metro-header--none { display: none; }
  .metro-wicon { width: 28px; height: 28px; }
  .metro-mark { width: 30px; height: 30px; }
  .metro-header--large .metro-mark { width: 42px; height: 42px; }
  .metro-header--tiny .metro-mark { width: 22px; height: 22px; }
  .metro-header--large .metro-wicon { width: 40px; height: 40px; }
  /* A solid ink badge with the text knocked out of it. On e-ink this is the
     highest-contrast mark available, and it gives the header two fixed
     anchors — what day it is, and how much of it is on screen — that read at
     a glance from across a room instead of as grey annotations. */
  .metro-pill {
    display: inline-block; white-space: nowrap;
    padding: 2px 10px; border-radius: 999px;
    /* one declaration for both pills: the linter counts property NAMES in
       the markup, and every extra one is spend against a small budget */
    background: var(--framework-semantic-text-primary-text-color, #000);
    color: var(--framework-semantic-canvas-bg-color, #fff);
    line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .metro-pill--quiet {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--framework-semantic-text-primary-text-color, #000);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px var(--framework-semantic-text-primary-text-color, #000);
  }
  .metro-terminus { white-space: nowrap; }
  .metro-sky { white-space: nowrap; }
  .metro-hour, .metro-axis-note, .metro-terminus, .metro-sky { padding: 1px 3px; }
  /* no vertical padding: see the line-height note above. The horizontal
     padding is the paper the words sit on and stays. */
  .metro-label { padding: 0 3px; }
  /* Ink mixed toward the canvas: a grey in either theme, at full opacity so
     the paper outline behind the glyphs stays solid. Where color-mix is not
     understood the declaration is dropped and the text is simply full ink,
     which is legible either way round. */
  .metro-quiet { color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--framework-semantic-text-primary-text-color, #000) 68%, var(--framework-semantic-canvas-bg-color, #fff)); }
  /* the badge is its own background, so it wants no paper outline: white
     text with a white outline behind it is a white blob */
  .metro-axis-note.metro-pill { padding: 1px 9px; -webkit-text-stroke: 0; }
</style>

<script>
(function () {
  // ON `window`, AND THEN ALIASED, so the minifier cannot rename it.
  //
  // push.sh squeezes this script on its way to the device, and the squeeze
  // now renames identifiers, which is 22KB on a file that had run out of
  // room under the server's 100KB limit. A renamed `METRO` would have cost
  // the layout suite its ability to swap a fixture into the artefact that
  // actually ships and measure THAT rather than the source. A property name
  // is not an identifier: `window.METRO` survives the mangler untouched,
  // and the local alias below can be renamed to a letter for all anyone
  // cares, because nothing outside this script looks for it.
  window.METRO = {{ metro | json }};
  var METRO = window.METRO;
  var SCRIPT = document.currentScript;
  var ROOT = SCRIPT && SCRIPT.parentElement ? SCRIPT.parentElement : document;
  var canvas = ROOT.querySelector('.metro-canvas');
  var svg = ROOT.querySelector('.metro-svg');
  var header = ROOT.querySelector('.metro-header');
  if (!canvas || !svg) return;

  var SVG_NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
  // The two attribute names the drawing writes most, named once: the
  // built copy has to fit the server's 100KB per-file limit and these are
  // 49 repeats of seventeen characters.
  var ROLE_ATTR = 'data-metro-role', OWNER_ATTR = 'data-metro-owner';
  var LEGEND = METRO.legend || [];
  // HOW A LINE IS DRAWN IS THE FRONT END'S BUSINESS.
  //
  // The payload used to carry a stroke width, a dash pattern and a hue for
  // every track, which meant transform.js -- a thing that knows about
  // calendars, events and weather -- was deciding what a metro line looks
  // like. It is the same rule that keeps the word "siding" out of that
  // file: the back end says who was where and when, and this file decides
  // how that is drawn.
  //
  // A pinned colour still comes through, because that is a preference
  // somebody set, not a drawing decision. Everything else is assigned here,
  // in board order, so the encoding is one table in one place:
  //
  //   the anchor   solid
  //   then         heavy dash, dotted, long dash-dot, wide dash
  //
  // at one stroke weight for all of them. Weight used to be the other half
  // of telling lines apart and it made the board look like some people's
  // days mattered more than others.
  // ...AND WHICH ENCODING DEPENDS ON HOW MANY GREYS THE PANEL HAS.
  //
  //   1-bit  there is one ink. The pattern is the whole identity, so all
  //          four textures are used and every line is full black.
  //   2-bit  three greys to hand. Grey and pattern together, so two lines
  //          never share both.
  //   4-bit  sixteen greys. A tonal ladder reads more quietly than four
  //          textures at this weight, so the lines go SOLID at descending
  //          tones and the dash patterns are kept back for the corridors,
  //          where they say "these two are running together" rather than
  //          "this is Bart".
  //
  // Called AFTER the bit depth is read. Written as an IIFE up here it ran
  // before `BITS` had a value -- `var` hoists the name and not the number --
  // and every panel got the 1-bit table. That is the third time in this
  // file a declaration below its reader has cost an afternoon.
  var TRACK_STYLES = ['heavydash', 'dotted', 'dashdot', 'widedash'];
  var TONES = ['black', 'gray-25', 'gray-40', 'gray-55', 'gray-70'];
  function styleTracks() {
    var n = 0;
    LEGEND.forEach(function (p) {
      // THE WEIGHT NEVER MOVES. Every spatial number on this board is
      // derived from it, so a panel with more greys must not have thinner
      // rails: see the note on BITS.
      p.line_width = 3;
      var tone = BITS === 1 ? 'black' : TONES[Math.min(n, TONES.length - 1)];
      if (!p.hue) p.hue = p.anchor ? 'black' : tone;
      p.line_style = p.anchor ? 'solid'
        : BITS >= 4 ? 'solid'
        : TRACK_STYLES[n % TRACK_STYLES.length];
      if (!p.anchor) n++;
    });
    var any = LEGEND.some(function (p) { return p.line_style === 'solid'; });
    if (!any && LEGEND.length) LEGEND[0].line_style = 'solid';
  }
  var DAY_START = METRO.day_start_min, DAY_END = METRO.day_end_min;

  function pad2(n) { return (n < 10 ? '0' : '') + n; }
  var HOUR12 = !!METRO.hour12;
  var I18N = METRO.i18n || { today: 'Today', more: '+{n} more', earlier: '+{n} earlier' };
  function tr(key, n) { return String(I18N[key] || key).replace('{n}', String(n)); }
  function timeLabel(min) {
    var h = Math.floor(min / 60) % 24, m = min % 60;
    if (!HOUR12) return pad2(h) + ':' + pad2(m);
    return (h % 12 || 12) + (m ? ':' + pad2(m) : '') + (h < 12 ? 'am' : 'pm');
  }
  // A RANGE INSIDE ONE HALF OF THE DAY SAYS am OR pm ONCE.
  //
  // "8am - 8:15am" prints the meridiem twice for a quarter of an hour, and
  // those four characters are not free: a caption's width is what the band
  // solver reserves room for through `markRoom`, so every label on the
  // board was booking space for a word it had already said. "8 - 8:15am"
  // loses nothing -- a range that starts in the morning and ends in the
  // morning is unambiguous from its end alone.
  //
  // Only within one half. Across noon or midnight both halves have to be
  // named or the range is a guess, and that is the one case where the
  // repetition is carrying information.
  function timeRange(a, b) {
    if (!(b > a)) return timeLabel(a);
    if (!HOUR12) return timeLabel(a) + ' \u2013 ' + timeLabel(b);
    var ha = Math.floor(a / 60) % 24, hb = Math.floor(b / 60) % 24;
    var lead = (ha < 12) === (hb < 12)
      ? timeLabel(a).replace(/(am|pm)$/, '')
      : timeLabel(a);
    return lead + ' \u2013 ' + timeLabel(b);
  }
  function hourLabel(h) { return HOUR12 ? ((h % 12 || 12) + (h < 12 ? 'am' : 'pm')) : pad2(h) + ':00'; }
  function el(tag, cls) { var e = document.createElement(tag); if (cls) e.className = cls; return e; }
  function svgEl(tag, attrs) { var e = document.createElementNS(SVG_NS, tag); for (var k in attrs) e.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]); return e; }
  function overlaps(a, b) { return a[0] < b[1] && b[0] < a[1]; }
  function clamp(v, lo, hi) { return Math.max(lo, Math.min(hi, v)); }

  // ---- device scale + paint ------------------------------------------
  function uiScale() {
    try { var v = TRMNLPaint.px(1, { el: canvas, kind: 'ui' }); if (isFinite(v) && v > 0) return v; } catch (e) {}
    return 1;
  }
  // On a 1-bit panel every line is solid black and the dash patterns tell
  // them apart — a dithered gray 3px line is just speckle. Grayscale panels
  // get the framework-resolved shade for each track's hue.
  var screenEl = canvas.closest('.screen');
  // HOW MANY GREYS THE PANEL HAS, and nothing else.
  //
  // Bit depth decides what a line is PAINTED with. It decides nothing about
  // where anything is: the stroke weight is the same three pixels on every
  // panel, so `laneBase`, `markRoom`, the isolation ring and every caption
  // position come out identical whatever this says. That is the whole point
  // of reading it here and only here -- a board that rearranges itself
  // because the device has more greys is a different board, and the one
  // thing a calendar may not do is look like a different day.
  var BITS = screenEl && screenEl.classList.contains('screen--1bit') ? 1
           : screenEl && screenEl.classList.contains('screen--2bit') ? 2 : 4;
  var ONE_BIT = BITS === 1;
  styleTracks();
  // Crisp pixels where there are no greys to blend with, smooth vectors
  // where there are. Paint only: neither changes a coordinate.
  canvas.setAttribute('shape-rendering', BITS === 1 ? 'crispEdges' : 'geometricPrecision');
  // Ink and paper, as the THEME defines them, not as hex. A device in dark
  // mode paints a black canvas and white text; a marker filled with a
  // literal #fff was then a solid white blob where a hollow ring should be,
  // and its literal #000 outline was invisible. These two CSS variables swap
  // with the theme, so "hollow" means the canvas colour whatever that is and
  // "drawn" means the text colour. SVG presentation ATTRIBUTES do not accept
  // var(), so both are applied through .style.
  var INK = 'var(--framework-semantic-text-primary-text-color, #000)';
  var PAPER = 'var(--framework-semantic-canvas-bg-color, #fff)';
  function paint(node, fill, stroke) {
    if (fill) node.style.fill = fill;
    if (stroke) node.style.stroke = stroke;
    return node;
  }
  var colorCache = {};
  function colorFor(hue) {
    // "black" is a role, not a colour. A line assigned literal black
    // vanished on a dark board — the anchor track, the heaviest line on the
    // map, drawn in the same ink as the canvas. Both ends of the greyscale
    // resolve through the theme instead, so the anchor is whatever "fully
    // inked" means on that device.
    if (hue === 'black') return INK;
    if (hue === 'white') return PAPER;
    if (ONE_BIT) return INK;
    if (!(hue in colorCache)) {
      var c = null;
      try { var f = TRMNLPaint.stroke(hue, { el: canvas }); c = f && f.color; } catch (e) {}
      colorCache[hue] = c || INK;
    }
    return colorCache[hue];
  }
  // ---- line treatments -------------------------------------------------
  //
  // Every line is SOLID and thick. A dashed line is a weak mark on e-ink:
  // it is mostly paper, so it reads as faint however dark the ink is, and at
  // the sizes this board uses a dotted line all but disappears against the
  // hour labels. Lines are told apart instead the way a printed transit map
  // does it — by weight, and by what is knocked OUT of the stroke:
  //
  //   plain   a solid bar
  //   casing  a paper stripe down the middle, so it reads as two parallel
  //           lines held together
  //   hatch   paper rungs across it at a regular pitch, the railway hatch
  //   beads   paper dots down the middle at a wider pitch
  //
  // All four keep a continuous black envelope, so they are equally heavy at
  // a glance and equally legible at 1-bit; what differs is the texture
  // inside. The overlay is drawn in the CANVAS colour over the same path, so
  // it inverts with the theme along with everything else.
  //
  // The `line_style` field still carries the four names the config and the
  // transform use; this is where they stop meaning "dash pattern".
  //
  // A treated line is drawn WIDER than a plain one by `widen`, because what
  // the eye weighs is the ink, not the envelope: knocking a paper core out
  // of a 4px line leaves about 1.3px of ink on each side and it reads as a
  // hairline beside a solid neighbour. Widening puts the ink back, so all
  // four treatments carry the same visual weight at the same nominal size
  // and the weight ladder means what it says.
  function treatmentFor(style, S, width) {
    var w = width * S * weightScale;
    var thin = Math.max(1 * S, 1.4 * S * weightScale);
    // FIVE ENCODINGS, ONE FOR EACH LINE, AT ONE WEIGHT.
    //
    // A line is drawn solid and a PAPER overlay is laid over it in the same
    // geometry, so what the pattern below describes is where the paper
    // goes: the ink shows through the GAPS in it. Every pattern here is
    // written as the ink the eye is meant to see and then inverted, which
    // is why the numbers look back to front -- `dash: "2 4"` is two of
    // paper and four of ink, and reads as a four-long dash.
    //
    // `core` is as wide as the line plus a margin, so the paper cuts all
    // the way through rather than leaving a rail either side of it. That
    // matters: a treatment that leaves two thin rails reads as two lines
    // running together, and two lines running together is what a shared
    // event is drawn as.
    //
    // Sized in device pixels (times S), at one stroke weight for every
    // line, so what tells them apart is the pattern and nothing else.
    //
    //   Marge   heavy dash    4 ink, 2 gap
    //   Bart    dotted        2 ink, 4 gap
    //   Lisa    long dash-dot 8 ink, 3 gap, 2 ink, 3 gap
    //   Maggie  wide dash     6 ink, 6 gap
    //
    // Homer is solid and has no treatment at all: the anchor line is the
    // one the eye should find first, and unbroken ink is how it does.
    var cut = w + 2 * S;
    if (style === 'heavydash') return { widen: 0, core: cut, cap: 'butt',
                                        dash: (2 * S) + ' ' + (4 * S) };
    // BUTT, NOT ROUND. The cap belongs to the PAPER, not to the ink, and a
    // round cap on a four-long stroke of paper reaches a stroke-width past
    // each end of it -- which at this weight is the whole two of ink
    // between them. Bart's line was drawn and then completely painted out:
    // a terminal bar at each end of the day and nothing in between.
    if (style === 'dotted') return { widen: 0, core: cut, cap: 'butt',
                                     dash: (4 * S) + ' ' + (2 * S) };
